{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall not destroy its trees by wielding an axe against them.</b> You shall not destroy the trees in order to swing an axe against them merely for destruction — not for any purpose, but only to harm the people of the city." "<b>For from it you shall eat.</b> For in truth, cutting down the trees as an act of mere destruction is done only by an army that intends to cause harm, when it is not confident of conquering and settling the land. But you, who are confident of conquering the land and settling in it, have no business destroying a food-bearing tree." "<b>For from it you shall eat.</b> That you will conquer the land without doubt and will eat from it when you do not destroy it." "<b>For the human is the tree of the field.</b> For is the tree of the field truly a human, that it should come before you because of the besieged city, to surrender itself into your hand by force of siege? And since this is not so here, and moreover even if it is fitting to harm the city's inhabitants with instruments of war — such as siege-ramps and the like — to bring the city under siege, behold, since you will not achieve this by destroying the trees, it is not fitting to destroy them — just as it is fitting that you would destroy the human inhabitants of the city." "<b>To come before you in the siege.</b> So that on account of this the city may come before you in siege, such that they surrender themselves into your hand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:21:54.064156Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}